Optional reference decision:
  The upstream ad-cs skill had only one reference file (vulnerabilities.md). The upstream
  SKILL.md itself contained substantial PKI architecture content (CA hierarchy, templates,
  enrolment, revocation, hardening checklist, troubleshooting table). This content was
  substantial enough to warrant a separate architecture.md reference rather than leaving it
  all in SKILL.md. The split keeps SKILL.md thin (~165 lines) and the architecture
  reference self-contained. Two references total: architecture.md (new; split from SKILL.md
  body content) and vulnerabilities.md (direct from upstream reference file).

Customisations applied:
  - Re-authored all agent-framing ("You are a specialist", "Identity and Scope",
    "How to Approach Tasks", "Assess vulnerability exposure") into skill voice.
  - Four-field vault frontmatter only: name, description, license MIT,
    metadata.version 1.0.0 (unquoted). Dropped upstream name (security-iam-ad-cs)
    and quoted version.
  - Skill marker in SKILL.md only; references carry no frontmatter and no marker
    (line 1 is the # Title).
  - British/Pacific English: enrolment (not enrollment in prose; enrollment kept verbatim in
    PowerShell/UI strings), organis*, authoris*, licence (noun). Product names (Certify,
    Certipy, Locksmith, PSPKIAudit, certutil, certsvc, EDITF_ATTRIBUTESUBJECTALTNAME2,
    ESC1-ESC16, PKINIT, NDES) kept verbatim.
  - Em-dash (U+2014) and en-dash (U+2013): zero occurrences.

Boundary notes:
  ad-cs = enterprise PKI built on AD CS: CA hierarchy, certificate templates, enrolment,
  revocation, ESC attack paths, hardening. PKINIT and smart card authentication depend on
  AD CS but the directory layer (Kerberos protocol, FSMO, replication) belongs to ad-ds.
  AD FS token-signing certificates are issued by AD CS but AD FS operations belong to ad-fs.
  CA private key storage and HSM credentials route to secrets-hygiene.

